<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro de Alvarado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1485–<a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro de Alvarado was a Spanish conquistador and lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, after which he subdued Tututepec. In 1524 he led the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, defeating the K'iche' with Kaqchikel allies and then turning on the Kaqchikel, and campaigned against the Tz'utujil and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> of Cuzcatlan. As governor of Guatemala he was notorious for cruelty. He died in 1541 after being crushed by a horse in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado.jpg" alt="Pedro de Alvarado" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomás Povedano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_de_Alvarado_(Tom%C3%A1s_Povedano).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tlaxcalan troops went on to fight in Spanish campaigns across Mesoamerica, including the conquest of Guatemala led by <b>Pedro de Alvarado</b> in the 1520s and the Mixtón War of 1540–1542.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pedro de Alvarado</b> conquered the K’iche' kingdom in 1524 and burned its capital, Q’umarkaj; the Montejos founded Mérida in 1542 and crushed the Great Maya Revolt of 1546–1547; and the Itza kingdom of Nojpetén, on an island in Lake Petén Itzá, held out until Martín de Ursúa stormed…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>…generally believed that Ximénez worked from a phonetic manuscript borrowed from a parishioner, a rendering of an oral recitation given in or around Santa Cruz del Quiché soon after <b>Pedro de Alvarado</b>'s conquest of 1524; yet, as Néstor Quiroa points out, no such manuscript has ever been found.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pedro de Alvarado</b> conquered the highland Maya kingdoms from 1524 and founded Santiago de los Caballeros, first at Iximche.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-guatemala">Captaincy General of Guatemala</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1524 <b>Pedro de Alvarado</b> invaded and fought the Pipil at Acajutla and Tacuzcalco, but they resisted for years; San Salvador, first founded in 1525, was re-established in 1528.</blockquote><a href="/state/cuzcatlan">Cuzcatlan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kaqchikel kings Kaji' Imox and Beleheb' Kat welcomed <b>Pedro de Alvarado</b> in 1524, and Iximche briefly became the first Spanish capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaqchikel-kingdom">Kaqchikel kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Guadalajara, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.88,-6.97&amp;year=1521" aria-label="Badajoz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4377 769 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro de Alvarado"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4827 1039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4827 1039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.88,-6.97&amp;year=1521">What was here in 1521? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
